{
  "requirement": "Repository validation MUST continue after a failed check and report every failed\ncheck it finds in one run, each as a {term}`requirements.ci.validation_error`.\nThis is a convention for every validation error; the requirement for each check\nstates which conditions that check reports.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "**Verdict:** `requirements.ci.errors` is satisfied. The implementation is marked and tested. I found no unmarked implementation and no unspecified behavior.\n\n**What the requirement asks for.** Validation must continue after a failed check. It must report every failed check it finds in one run, each as a validation error. Per-check requirements say which conditions each check reports.\n\n**Implementation.** `inspect_repository` and `inspect_repository_with_progress` in `src/evidence.rs` (lines 157–295) carry `{impl}` for this requirement. They run every phase in sequence against one shared `errors` vector. None of the phases stops the run when it finds a failure:\n- **Workspace config.** `load_workspace_evidence` returns a `Result`, and an `Err` is pushed to `errors`, not propagated with `?`.\n- **Book source and definitions.** `book_source_dir` pushes its errors and falls back to `md`. `collect_definitions` pushes errors for invalid names, missing fences, bad options, empty text, duplicates and unreferenced examples. It then uses `continue` to move on to the next block or file.\n- **Evidence files.** Each file goes through `validate_evidence_file` (and `validate_input` for its inputs), which push one message per problem. A `return` in these helpers (unreadable file, invalid JSON, non-object value) only ends that one file. The loop moves on to the next file, and later checks (missing coverage, reference validation, Rust citation coverage) still run.\n- **Missing evidence and references.** The missing-evidence loop, `collect_references_with_progress`, `validate_references` and `rust_coverage::validate_rust_functions` also push and continue.\n- **Aborts.** The only early aborts are `io::Error`s from `fs::canonicalize(root)` and similar, which are not failed validation checks.\n\n`DefinitionKind::noun` in `src/lib.rs` (lines 60–69) is marked `{impl}`. It supplies the \"requirement\", \"example\" or \"definition\" wording in the messages. It adds no behavior beyond that.\n\n**Message shape.** Messages begin with the affected file, source location or definition. For example, `\"{label}: invalid JSON: …\"`, `\"{source}: duplicate …; first declared at …\"` and `\"evidence/{name}.json: missing for … at …\"`. They name the failed check and give the specific value, which matches `requirements.ci.validation_error`. The detailed content of each message belongs to the per-check requirements (`ci.diagnostics` and others), which are separately marked.\n\n**Tests.**\n- `collects_missing_orphan_format_false_and_changed_line_errors` (`src/evidence.rs`, lines 1507–1608) has a `{test}` reference. It builds one repository with many simultaneous faults: a duplicate requirement, an empty requirement, a changed requirement text, a false determination, a stale outline hash, bad input fields, a missing outline node, wrong-type fields, an orphan evidence file, invalid JSON, and missing evidence. It asserts that all roughly 20 distinct errors appear in a single `validate_repository` run. This is the core scenario for the requirement.\n- `inspection_progress_preserves_validation_and_finishes_each_phase` (lines 1304–1396) has a `{test}` reference. Its invalid case combines invalid JSON, an unparsable Rust file and an uncited function. It asserts that all three errors are reported together and that the validation phases still run.\n\nTogether these cover both the \"keep going after a failure\" and the \"report all failures in one run\" points of the design space. Failures from several different phases are asserted in the same run.\n\nNo acknowledged gaps (FIXME) are present, and I found none that needed one.",
